hadiths[count]{hadithnumber,text}:
"208","On the authority of Bundar, on the authority of Muhammad ibn Jaafar, on the authority of Shu’bah, on the authority of Amr ibn Murrah, who said: I heard Abdullah ibn Salamah, he said: I entered upon Ali ibn Abu Talib and I and two men: a man from among us, and a man from Banu Asad, I think. So he sent them back and said: You are both sick, so treat your religion. Then he entered the exit and then came out, so he took a handful of water and wiped himself with it, then he came and recited the Qur’an completely, but we denied that, so Ali said: “ The Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, used to come to the toilet and relieve himself, then go out and eat bread and meat with us, and recite The Qur’an, and nothing prevents him from the Qur’an except impurity, or anything other than impurity.” He said: I heard Ahmad bin Al-Miqdam Al-Ajli saying: Sa’id bin Al-Rabi’ narrated to us, on the authority of Shu’bah, with this hadith. Shu’bah said: “ This is a third of my capital.” Abu Bakr said: I had made it clear in the Book of Sales that there is a difference between what is disliked and what is forbidden, and I demonstrated the difference between them by the saying of the Prophet May God’s prayers and peace be upon him: “Indeed, God hates three things for you, and forbids three for you. He hates gossip, asking too many questions, and wasting money, and forbids “On you is disobedience to mothers, infanticide of daughters, and withholding and giving.” He differentiated between the reprehensible and the forbidden by saying in the report of Al-Muhajir ibn Qunfud: “ “I hate to remember God except in a state of purity.” It may be possible that he only disliked that, since the remembrance of God is in a state of purity is better, not that the remembrance of God is in a manner other than that. Purity is forbidden, since the Prophet, may God’s prayers and peace be upon him, used to recite the Qur’an while not in purity, and the Qur’an is the best of remembrance, and the Prophet, may God’s prayers and peace be upon him, used to He, peace and blessings be upon him, remembers God at all times, according to what we narrated on the authority of Aisha, may God be pleased with her. It may be that his hatred for the remembrance of God Except in a state of purity, remembrance of God, which is obligatory upon a person and not something voluntary. If remembrance of God is obligatory, he does not perform the obligation in any other way. He is purified until he becomes pure, then he fulfills the obligation of purity, because returning the greeting is an obligation according to most scholars, and he, may God’s prayers and peace be upon him, did not respond. He was not pure until he became pure, then he returned the greeting. As for what a person did voluntarily in remembrance of God, even if he left it in a state he was in, If he is not pure, he does not have to repeat it. He has the right to remember God voluntarily, even if he is not pure."
